import Foundation

enum SweepHelp {
    static let parameters = HelpTopic(title: "Sweep parameters", summary: "Each axis changes one setting from the Generate tab's base configuration. ERP axes require the editable ERP experiment.", guidance: "Values are absolute settings, except background gain, which multiplies the base EEG amplitudes. Gradient amplitude preserves the base min/max ratio. BCG morphology enables the generator model. Artifact amplitude axes enable their artifact; blink amplitude requires a nonzero blink rate. Bad-channel fraction rounds to a channel count and requires no explicitly named bad channels. Select Example values to fill units-appropriate values.")
    static let values = HelpTopic(title: "Sweep values and ranges", summary: "Enter a comma-separated list, such as 0, 15, 30, or start:stop:step, such as 0:60:15. The stop is included when the step reaches it.", guidance: "Fractions use 0–1, not percentages. Values must be finite and distinct; a range can have at most 1000 values. Each grid point is validated before recording generation begins.")
    static let scope = HelpTopic(title: "Condition and peak scope", summary: "Condition settings can target one condition or all conditions. Peak settings can target one peak within a selected condition, all its peaks, or all peaks across conditions.", guidance: "Response jitter, independent peak jitter, amplitude/width variation, noise, omissions, and scheduling are currently shared experiment settings. Condition-share sweeps redistribute other shares proportionally. Mixture sweeps change only the selected recipe fraction and consume the Typical remainder; invalid totals fail validation.")
    static let grid = HelpTopic(title: "Two-axis grid", summary: "Cross every primary-axis value with every second-axis value. These are repeated simulations of one subject, not a group cohort.", guidance: "Total recordings = first-axis values × second-axis values × seed repetitions. Use different parameters for the axes. Results plot one line for each second-axis value. The complete grid must be valid; for example, every ISI must exceed its jitter range.")
    static let seeds = HelpTopic(title: "Repeated seeds", summary: "Each repetition gets a deterministic seed. That same seed is reused at every grid point, pairing runs across values.", guidance: "The first repetition uses the Generate-tab seed; further seeds are derived from it. Independent simulation streams preserve unrelated draws where possible, but changing schedule, duration, or legacy model structure can change realized samples. Repetitions quantify simulation variability, not between-subject variability.")
    static let duration = HelpTopic(title: "Sweep recording duration", summary: "Extend each editable-ERP run as needed to include its actual seeded responses and evaluation post-stimulus epochs. Never shortens the base recording.", guidance: "This is useful when varying trial count or ISI. Turn it off to keep the base duration fixed; responses that do not fit cause preflight validation to fail. Epochs missing a pre-stimulus baseline are excluded and counted separately.")
    static let evaluation = HelpTopic(title: "Generate + Evaluate", summary: "ERP average epochs every condition and compares its noisy-recording average with the matching clean-recording average. Reject + average additionally rejects noisy epochs above an amplitude threshold.", guidance: "Epochs are unfiltered and baseline-corrected using their pre-stimulus samples. The clean reference uses all complete epochs, including omissions, background EEG, and overlap. The estimate uses retained noisy epochs. Errors therefore include distortion from trial selection. This does not run artifact correction or classify trial recipes. All settings and per-run counts are saved with results.")
    static let windows = HelpTopic(title: "Evaluation windows", summary: "Pre and post specify epoch lengths in seconds around each stimulus. The pre-stimulus mean is subtracted separately from each epoch/channel. Incomplete epochs are excluded.", guidance: "Waveform RMSE and correlation use all EEG channels over the whole post-stimulus window. Peak start/end select an interval within that window. Keep windows fixed across the sweep to make comparisons interpretable.")
    static let peaks = HelpTopic(title: "Peak evaluation", summary: "Peak measurements use the selected one-based EEG channel and the largest absolute deflection within the search window, preserving its sign.", guidance: "The channel stays fixed across runs. Errors are measured minus clean, in µV and ms. Use a narrow window for a particular component; the largest deflection can switch between components in a broad window. A zero clean response has no defined peak, and a zero measured response has no defined peak latency.")
    static let rejection = HelpTopic(title: "Epoch rejection", summary: "Reject an entire trial when any EEG channel's unfiltered peak-to-peak amplitude exceeds this threshold in µV. The decision uses noisy data only.", guidance: "This is an explicit simple rejection pipeline, not the TrialsView similarity classifier. High-amplitude ERP responses can also be rejected. The clean reference includes rejected trials so selection bias remains visible. If no trials survive, recovery metrics are undefined rather than zero.")
    static let results = HelpTopic(title: "Sweep results", summary: "Plots show the mean and sample SD across repeated seeds for each cell. Choose a condition and metric. n counts valid finite results; one repetition has no SD.", guidance: "RMSE is in µV; correlation is Pearson correlation over post-stimulus channel/sample pairs. Peak errors are signed measured-minus-clean differences. Retained fraction uses complete epochs as its denominator. Infinite broadband SNR and undefined ERP metrics appear as missing values in JSON. Per-run evaluation.json, scenario.json, the aggregate CSV, and sweep_results.json preserve settings, seeds, counts, and results. Error bars are spread, not confidence intervals.")
}
